1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,800
It's, um, I was, I was actually in Silverstone for,

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,100
I think it was Friday practice, and I went back to Finland and, uh, on

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:13,380
Sunday morning, and, uh, I saw Michael's accident on the TV in the airport.

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:20,140
And, uh, I didn't think much of it and, uh, because it didn't look big, so I, I was just hoping he's fine.

5
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:25,880
And, uh, then a couple hours later I heard he broke his leg and, uh, didn't think anything.

6
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,680
So, uh, I was getting married during the next week.

7
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,700
We had a, like, a wedding rehearsal on Monday, and then I had a phone call from Jean Todt.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:41,240
And, uh, he called me when we were practicing this wedding and, uh, and, um,

9
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:51,740
he said, uh, "I want you to come to Maranello." And I thought, "Oh, okay." Uh, I- of course immediately I can connect these two things and, uh, he, uh...

10
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:54,460
I asked, "When?" He said, "Now."

11
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:57,060
So he did not give me option.

12
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:01,260
I said, "I'm my wed- I'm in my wedding re- rehearsal." And he said, "It doesn't matter.

13
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:04,520
You come to Maranello now." Two hours later I was in airplane.

14
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:12,140
So, uh, to take the wheel of the car for Michael Schumacher is not easy because it is a- he had all his crew behind him. Uh,

15
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:22,260
it was a particular moment, I think a, a difficult moment for you to, to, to adapt at, at his style of, of driving, for example?

16
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:26,180
Yeah, it was... Ferrari was very kind to me when I went there.

17
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,080
It was, I felt very welcome from day one.

18
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:36,960
We had a really good time of staying in Jean's house in Maranello, and, uh, Jean, Jean is like a workaholic. He works non-stop.

19
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:40,880
Uh, he sleeps, like, two hours a night, and then he works all the other time, so I never saw him.

20
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:50,840
And, uh, I was getting very hungry in his house because there was no food, so, uh, Ross Brawn brought me some food in the evening to eat and, uh, but, uh, I felt so welcomed from beginning.

21
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:58,680
And, uh, every time I went to factory and everybody was helping me, and I had phone calls with Michael also, and Michael helped me how to adapt in the team quickly.

22
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,620
Quickly and because next race was already one week later.

23
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:04,080
So he was supportive on this time to, to help you to find-

24
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:05,480
... the best way to adapt to the car.

25
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:12,520
I, I talked a lot with Michael on the phone and, uh, we had video calls and, uh, and, uh, he was helping me a lot, um,

26
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:20,520
to teach me about the people in team, who I need to go if I need something specific for the car.

27
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:26,840
So he told me the names which are who I need to talk and, uh, it just, uh, it, it was very, very easy to adapt.

28
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:32,600
And of course I knew Eddie from before also, so it was quite easy, easy to get help from him also.

29
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:40,880
And you, you were quickly competitive because some, uh, weeks a- after that in Germany you are close to, to win.

30
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:47,560
Yes. Um, first race was Austria and, uh, it was sort of okay, but, uh,

31
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,720
I did got no testing at all straight there.

32
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:56,780
And then after Austria we came here to Mugello for I think three days. I drove non-stop from morning.

33
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,160
We could play a little bit with the car and it was more comfortable for me, and then in Germany I could have won it.

34
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:09,140
And it was a particular moment for you because, uh, you, you were in ahead and close to win, and you have a call on the radio.

35
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:10,860
I don't know if it was Ross Brawn or Jean Todt-

36
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:17,780
... who asked you to let Eddie Irvine to, to win because he was, uh, uh, fighting for the championship.

37
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:21,640
I presume it was a very, uh, tough moment for you.

38
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:25,560
Yeah, but we, we talked about this, uh, before my first race.

39
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:32,880
Uh, Jean gave me, like, a two-hour lesson how important it is that I help team and Eddie only to win the championship because I have no chance to win.

40
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:37,200
So, uh, if this situation comes up that Eddie is behind me, I need to let him pass.

41
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:44,880
And so I knew of course the whole Hockenheim race when I was leading, I had, uh, Heinz-Harald Frentzen behind me. I s- it was yellow car.

42
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:47,920
I was all the time hoping, "Yes, stay there. Stay there." And then

43
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:55,220
Eddie managed to pass Heinz during pit stops, and then I saw red car behind me and I thought, "Oh, okay,

44
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:04,700
and now I know what's gonna happen next." And then very soon came the radio call from Ross Brawn with his calm voice that, "Mika, I want you to let Eddie pass this lap."

45
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:06,940
Yeah. And you sacrificed your-

46
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:18,340
And you f- sacrificed your first victory for, for the, the happiness of the Scuderia and of- also for the giving the maximum chance for Eddie Irvine.

47
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:22,880
Uh, uh, you have a, a special relationship with, uh, with, uh, Michael Schumacher on this time.

48
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:29,140
We, um, like a fan, we, uh, we have the vision of a, a driver who is very, uh,

49
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,720
implicated in, uh, very serious in, in his jobs.

50
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:40,160
And all people we met today in Mugello said he, he was a, a so kind driver, a so kind a gentleman in the life.

51
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:45,060
Yeah, he was nice guy. We, we knew each other from karting already, and we used to race karting.

52
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:46,900
We did, uh, Formula Ford together.

53
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:52,820
Actually, I beat him in Formula Ford quite, mm, I fin- won the European Championship, but Michael finished second.

54
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:59,280
And, uh, Formula 3 we were, uh, Macau Grand Prix, Michael won, I was second, and Eddie third.

55
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:01,800
So it's, we, we've been, we've been growing up-

56
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:03,040
It's a long story between you and him

57
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:06,080
... we've been growing up together, so I know him from a long time.

58
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:16,860
Yeah. And after this, uh, experience you have a, um, a vision different of Formula One because it was maybe the, the most important, uh, uh, team, uh,

59
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:19,900
for who you drive on this time.

60
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:25,440
I know you, you have experiences with, uh, with, uh, Toyota, you experiences with other crew.

61
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:30,380
Uh, but it was a different strategy, different organization.

62
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:32,420
It's a big, big factory.

63
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,880
It's really a, a different culture, DNA in the Ferrari.

64
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:37,960
Yeah, it was my first, like, big team.

65
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:46,800
I was in Tyrrell and Arrows and, uh, smaller teams before, so it was first, like, a big team and, uh, of course it opened my eyes for many things. Uh,

66
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:56,780
how quick they can react for if you need something for the car and, uh, they would do it immediately, not think about it for two weeks, and, uh, it was in the car immediately.

67
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:02,700
And the factory was working non-stop and, uh, the resources they have, and we were testing so much.

68
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:05,720
We had a different test team waiting every time after the race.

69
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:09,000
We went to s- different circuits and there was a test team waiting and, uh-

70
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,420
So many laps and so much testing.

71
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:17,520
Sometimes I got the call in the middle of the night in Maranello and some engineer found something in the dampers.

72
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:20,560
So I went to the middle of the night, they woke me up and come here.

73
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:24,840
I went there and did three laps over the curbs and then go back to sleep.

74
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:28,400
This track here in Mugello has a particular

75
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:37,460
importance in the success of the Scuderia this time because to have a private

76
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,840
practice track, it was an element key for victory.

77
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:45,020
Yeah, we did a lot of laps here in Mugello and

78
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:53,860
it's a very challenging track for the car setup wise. And there's every type of corners. There's high speed corners and

79
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:57,580
there's some bumps and you get everything working here really well.

80
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,680
If the car works here, it will work everywhere.

81
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:07,280
And this experience for Ferrari, I believe was wonderful for you because it gave you

82
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:13,480
a great exposition, worldwide exposition with your amazing results.

83
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:17,060
It was a chance for you to go to Toyota behind.

84
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:19,580
It's Ferrari who gave you this opportunity to go to Toyota?

85
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:24,640
Yeah, sort of. I went to Sauber first because they had a Ferrari engine. So

86
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:29,100
John wanted to put me in Sauber to stay in the family, kind of.

87
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:34,240
But then Toyota came up after that and Toyota was like

88
00:07:35,780 --> 00:07:37,900
my chance to get in another big team.

89
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:40,660
Only problem, Toyota had Ange Pasquale.

90
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:44,420
He was the only problem with the Toyota. No, it's a joke.

91
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:47,640
Yeah, I know. We say hello to Ange.

92
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:51,320
So thank you, Mika, for this interview.

93
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:55,540
